1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:07,500
(Q31（单选）) Today we're going to look at an important area of science, namely nanotechnology.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:17,800
So what is it? Nano means tiny, so it's science and engineering on the scale of atoms and molecules.

3
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:24,600
The idea is that by controlling and rearranging atoms, you can literally create anything.

4
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:34,000
However, as we'll see, the science of the small has some big implications affecting us in many ways.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,048
There's no doubt that nanotechnology promises so much for civilisation.

6
00:00:39,048 --> 00:00:43,594
However, all new technologies have their teething problems.

7
00:00:43,594 --> 00:00:50,399
[Q31答案句] And with nanotechnology, society often gets the wrong idea about its capabilities.

8
00:00:50,399 --> 00:01:11,543
Numerous science-fiction books and movies have raised people's fears about nanotechnology - with scenarios such as inserting little nano-robots into your body that monitor everything you do without you realising it, or self-replicating nano-robots that eventually take over the world.

9
00:01:11,543 --> 00:01:17,992
(Q32) So how do we safeguard such a potentially powerful technology?

10
00:01:17,992 --> 00:01:27,143
[Q32-A干扰项] Some scientists recommend that nano-particles be treated as new chemicals with separate safety tests and clear labelling.

11
00:01:27,143 --> 00:01:33,670
[Q32答案句] They believe that greater care should also be taken with nano-particles in laboratories and factories.

12
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:43,849
[Q32-C干扰项] Others have called for a withdrawal of new nano products such as cosmetics and a temporary halt to many kinds of nanotech research.

13
00:01:43,849 --> 00:01:51,409
(Q33) [Q33答案句] But as far as I'm concerned, there's a need to plough ahead with the discoveries and applications of nanotechnology.

14
00:01:51,409 --> 00:01:58,989
I really believe that most scientists would welcome a way to guard against unethical uses of such technology.

15
00:01:58,989 --> 00:02:05,143
We can't go around thinking that all innovation is bad, all advancement is bad.

16
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:11,449
As with the debate about any new technology, it is how you use it that's important.

17
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:19,538
(Q34（填空）) So let's look at some of its possible uses.

18
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:29,800
[Q34 答案句 metal] Thanks to nanotechnology, there could be a major breakthrough in the field of transportation with the production of more durable metals.

19
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:39,500
(Q35) These could be virtually unbreakable, lighter and much more pliable leading to planes that are 50 times lighter than at present.

20
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:52,500
[Q35 答案句 space] Those same improved capabilities will dramatically reduce the cost of travelling into space making it more accessible to ordinary people and opening up a totally new holiday destination.

21
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,633
(Q36) In terms of technology, the computer industry will be able to shrink computer parts down to minute sizes.

22
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:15,400
[Q36答案句] We need nanotechnology in order to create a new generation of computers that will work even faster and will have a million times more memory but will be about the size of a sugar cube.

23
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:29,800
(Q37) [Q37答案句] Nanotechnology could also revolutionise the way that we generate power. The cost of solar cells will be drastically reduced so harnessing this energy will be far more economical than at present.

24
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:39,000
(Q38) But nanotechnology has much wider applications than this and could have an enormous impact on our environment.

25
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:51,000
For instance, tiny airborne nano-robots could be programmed to actually rebuild the ozone layer, which could lessen the impact of global warming on our planet.

26
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,300
That's a pretty amazing thought, isn't it?

27
00:03:55,300 --> 00:04:09,800
[Q38答案句] On a more local scale, this new technology could help with the clean-up of environmental disasters as nanotechnology will allow us to remove oil and other contaminants from the water far more effectively.

28
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:22,199
(Q39) And, if nanotechnology progresses as expected - as a sort of building block set of about 90 atoms - then you could build anything you wanted from the bottom up.

29
00:04:22,199 --> 00:04:30,000
[Q39答案句] In terms of production, this means that you only use what you need and so there wouldn't be any waste.

30
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,199
(Q40) The notion that you could create anything at all has major implications for our health. “

31
00:04:36,199 --> 00:04:40,399
It means that we'll eventually be able to replicate anything.

32
00:04:40,399 --> 00:04:43,826
This would have a phenomenal effect on our society.

33
00:04:43,826 --> 00:04:52,480
In time it could even lead to the eradication of famine through the introduction of machines that produce food to feed the hungry.

34
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:59,600
But it's in the area of medicine that nanotechnology may have its biggest impact.

35
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:08,259
[Q40答案句] How we detect disease will change as tiny biosensors are developed to analyse tests in minutes rather than days.

36
00:05:08,259 --> 00:05:17,000
There's even speculation nano-robots could be used to slow the ageing process, lengthening life expectancy.

37
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,600
As you can see, I'm very excited by the implications that could be available to us in the next few decades.

38
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:27,100
Just how long it'll take, I honestly don't know.

